# The fleet's shared Python environment, built by bin/pyfleet with uv.
#
# Basic amenities for back-of-the-envelope work: check an order of magnitude,
# plot something quickly, poke at a table. NOT a research environment --
# anything heavy (samplers, ML, a stack pinned for one paper) gets its own uv
# environment, which is the right tool for that and keeps this one from becoming
# a dependency-resolution problem shared by every machine.
#
# Edit and run `pyfleet sync`. One per line, comments stripped, same format as
# packages/apt and packages/pacman.

numpy
scipy
threadpoolctl        # tiny, pure python; ergon doctor uses it to see which
                     # BLAS/OpenMP runtime numpy actually loaded (ERGON-63)
pandas
matplotlib
pillow               # ergon fig reads PNG metadata through it. matplotlib
                     # pulls it in anyway; naming it is what lets
                     # ergon-lint see that something provides it.

# Notebooks: marimo, and Jupyter only as the notebooks bundle (ERGON-54). A
# marimo notebook is a .py file, so it diffs, and execution is reactive, so
# there is no stale cell -- out-of-order execution is the largest single source
# of notebook results nobody can reproduce. That is the reason, not size: over the
# rest of this list marimo adds 171 MiB, jupyterlab + ipykernel + ipywidgets 132
# (uv, python 3.13, 2026-09-26). Not in the Arch repos; PyPI is the only route.
#
# Nothing else here needs ipykernel: ergon kernel installs it into the PROJECT
# env. A venv built before this gets marimo from install.sh's `pyfleet ensure`
# and keeps its jupyterlab, since nothing here removes a package. A clean base
# is `pyfleet destroy`, `pyfleet sync`, then `ergon bundle sync` for bundles.
marimo

# A command you type, and no longer something jupyterlab brings along.
ipython

# Units and constants. Back-of-the-envelope is exactly what astropy.units is
# for: dimensional arithmetic that catches a factor of 1000 before it reaches a
# plot. Nothing else here depends on it.
astropy
